WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.573 --> 00:00:03.240
(bright music)

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.080
- So Jake, how'd you get
here? What was childhood like?

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:31.740
- I was thinking how did I get
here like on my motorcycle?

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:34.680
Childhood was interesting.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.750
I spent a lot of time on the internet,

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:40.110
a lot of time on internet forums.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:43.650
So like two main forums that
I spend a lot of time on

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:48.120
was a like video game hacking
forums called This Game Sucks.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.510
And what actually ended up
being Palmer Luckey's forums,

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.550
like the ModRetro Forums.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:55.860
And so I spent a lot of time doing that.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.200
Thought I wanted to go in
electrical engineering.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.300
By the time I went to
college, I did my first year

00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.850
in electrical and then realized

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:03.930
I just wanted to be in software.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.700
The loop was just so much faster.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.540
- And how'd you get excited
about gaming and hacking?

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.390
How does that contribute to all this?

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.690
- I don't know what the
like core of it was.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.420
I just found it really,
really cool that you could,

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:19.800
I don't know, build stuff

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.140
or engage with various different people.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.683
So I'd spend a bunch of
time on that IRC channel,

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:30.000
hanging out with tons and
tons of different people.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.390
So I was probably like
13 or 15 at that time.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:35.850
And so I was working with people

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:39.360
on hacking various different consoles

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.310
with people who were like 35 years old

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:42.330
or anything else like that.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:45.270
And it was just like a
very, very cool universe.

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:48.120
It was like a very
multidimensional kind of like space

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.213
of just like, wow, there's so
much stuff I can like do here.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:56.040
And then a lot of the time
I would spend my time doing

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.420
the things that I needed to at school.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:58.350
And then I was like, okay, well,

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:00.303
I want to go back there basically.

00:02:01.916 --> 00:02:03.527
And so that's how I ended up
spending a lot of the time.

00:02:03.527 --> 00:02:06.930
- And was the beauty in gaming
and hacking the creative

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:08.880
and visual and aesthetic side

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.753
or was it the technical building
and deconstructing side?

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.090
- I think it was just fun to have a sort

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.220
of impact on a thing, like being able

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.430
to push it a little bit, like
write a small computer program

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:24.030
or like see how much you can
cut up like a Nintendo 64 board

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:25.563
before it like stops working.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:31.170
I think also tangentially,
my parents tried to get me

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:34.173
to be excited about a lot
of things like growing up.

00:02:35.027 --> 00:02:36.810
And I think they spent
a lot of time trying to

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:39.363
get me interested in various
different activities,

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:42.900
many of which like pretty close to none

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:44.790
that I was actually interested in.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:49.790
So I played piano growing
up, soccer, swimming,

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.130
golf, various different things.

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:54.900
And then when I kind of latched onto

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.660
the computer programming
or stuff like that,

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:00.160
I remember my dad was like, he bought me

00:03:01.522 --> 00:03:03.450
a soldering iron and a kit, right?

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:06.450
And he just realized that
that was more of the thing

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:08.490
that I wanted to be interested in.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:11.640
And so all these kind of ideas
that they sort of had for me,

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.440
they just kind of shifted a little bit

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:15.480
and it took them a little bit
of time to go in and do that.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:16.620
But those are the things

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:17.880
that I ended up gravitating towards.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:21.663
And then I had my parents be
like, "All right, do that.

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:25.830
Just lean into it and see how
well it goes or whatever."

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:30.830
- And when in your life
would you say your worldview

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:34.710
cemented around having a sense of mission

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:37.710
and sort of inward value versus outward

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:39.573
value and material things?

00:03:40.908 --> 00:03:41.741
Because I think it's quite remarkable.

00:03:41.741 --> 00:03:44.490
I mean, you now have
raised this massive hundred

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.500
million dollar round.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:49.530
You still own over a third of the company

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:52.950
and you're one of the most
low-key people I've ever met.

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:56.070
And I think from our conversations,

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:59.880
it sounds much more like
you want to build something

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.640
that takes it all, more
of a movement, a mission

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.940
than a company that does well,

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:09.150
that gets tied up in a
ribbon for an acquirer.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.100
- Yeah.
- Where does that come from?

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:12.720
- So I think it's probably a journey

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:14.130
that's also still cementing,

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:16.440
but I think the number one thing
that I could probably point

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:21.000
to is that when I was in
college, I worked remotely

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.593
for Wolfram Research.

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:27.090
And I wasn't all in on that.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.820
I was spending a lot of time
riding around on my motorcycle,

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:30.840
getting a bunch of work done,

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:35.220
and then really leaning into
the almost laissez-faire

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:37.680
kind of like, "Oh, I'm
gonna let work be work

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.350
and I'm gonna go and try and
live my life in general."

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:42.570
And then it got really, really boring.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:45.630
I had read the whole Tim
Ferriss four-hour work week

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:49.290
in general and I was like, I
mean, there's got to be more to

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:51.000
life than this in general.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:54.723
And I was like, okay, I want
to do something more, I guess,

00:04:56.230 --> 00:04:57.990
with this life I've got.

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:00.330
- Yeah, core to everything
I've gotten to know about you,

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:04.620
Jake, I think your level
of focus on conviction

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:08.130
is one of the most remarkable
that I've come across.

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:12.420
It runs through a lot of
different strands of your life,

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:14.040
personal and professional.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:16.110
On the professional side, you chose

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:19.440
to be a solo founder in
the face of data saying

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:20.490
that you need two or three

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:23.970
or four of you to have
a successful company.

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:26.880
You have a fully remote team
when the pendulum is swinging

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:29.070
back towards in-office stuff.

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:30.360
Some of your practices

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:35.010
of empowering your employees
are non-traditional,

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:38.280
the clarity and simplicity of your values,

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:42.150
but sort of the tall bar
of them is differentiated

00:05:42.150 --> 00:05:47.100
and you stick to them in
a pretty religious way.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:51.540
The whole thing is basically
a masterclass, I think,

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:52.980
in deep conviction.

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:56.190
And I'd love to understand more
about where that comes from

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:59.550
and how it benefits both
your life and Railway.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:03.660
- It's very, very difficult,
I think, to make large bets

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:06.300
and starting a startup is a
large bet if you don't really,

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:08.100
really believe the foundation on which you

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:09.933
are sitting basically.

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:13.260
And so for me, to develop that
conviction is actually like,

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.900
it's really, really, really important.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:18.930
And I have friends who
have started companies

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:21.640
with co-founders, and my
thesis has always been

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:28.170
it takes a while to develop
that conviction on your own.

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:30.360
And so if you have kind
of like another person

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:34.110
or a triad of people, the rate
at which your brain can fire

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:36.840
essentially and develop
consensus on its own is a million

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:38.730
times faster than trying to communicate

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:39.807
these things to other people.

00:06:39.807 --> 00:06:43.050
And so if I want to develop
that deep level of conviction,

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:46.470
it's really, really difficult
to do it with another person

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:49.380
that you actually need
consensus or three people on.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:52.830
And so that doesn't mean that
you can't make those things

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:55.500
and that I need to make
unilateral calls internally,

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:56.333
almost the opposite.

00:06:56.333 --> 00:06:59.724
I try and put myself into
a situation where there is

00:06:59.724 --> 00:07:00.720
what I call a triad of people.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.130
I can be outvoted in general,

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:04.140
but I should have enough
deep conviction on my own

00:07:04.140 --> 00:07:06.819
to be able to communicate those things.

00:07:06.819 --> 00:07:10.890
And so I think the YC
advice is reasonably good

00:07:10.890 --> 00:07:13.260
for most people.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:14.580
I think you're getting really,

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:17.167
really solid average
advice in terms of like,

00:07:17.167 --> 00:07:21.000
"Hey, listen, if you want a
successful enough company,

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.920
then you should go in and do this."

00:07:22.920 --> 00:07:26.610
But my goal has not been a
successful enough company.

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:29.130
It's as cringe as it sounds or whatever,

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:29.970
it's like the Steve Jobs,

00:07:29.970 --> 00:07:31.380
you want to dent the universe.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:34.680
And you're not gonna go in
and do that by taking advice

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:36.090
that is distilled from other people.

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:38.790
You're gonna go in and do
that by building your own

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:40.020
framework of knowledge that you're

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:42.030
gonna go in and operate on right?

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:44.460
People ask me for book recommendations

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:45.330
or anything else like that.

00:07:45.330 --> 00:07:47.530
And I have a few that
I really, really like,

00:07:48.464 --> 00:07:50.137
"The Courage to be Disliked"
is an excellent book,

00:07:50.137 --> 00:07:52.860
"High Output Management" by
Andy Grove, an excellent book.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:55.200
But I can't really tell you
anything off the top of my head

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.240
as to why those books are
excellent because I read them

00:07:57.240 --> 00:08:00.480
and I basically threw away
every single piece of lesson

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:03.060
and I tried to figure out
which things stuck over time.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:06.690
And as a result, I've kind
of compacted this almost like

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:10.710
heuristics engine into this
thing that I can navigate

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:13.530
and allows me to make
deep conviction bets.

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:16.650
And I think that you only
get to be able to do that

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:19.830
when you make a ton of
really big mistakes that hurt

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:23.070
and allow you to learn and process

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:24.540
and evolve those things over time.

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:28.080
- One of the things I love
when you talk about Railway is

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:30.240
for the team, it's okay to break shit,

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:33.750
but it's not okay to
be guilty of inaction.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:35.867
- Yeah.
- And I love that.

00:08:35.867 --> 00:08:36.700
And I also think,

00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:38.970
I wonder if there's a connection between

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:43.950
the sole foundership, the
focus on maxing out conviction,

00:08:43.950 --> 00:08:47.400
maxing agency, and the
way that you designed

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:48.570
and manage your team.

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:49.980
It's fully remote.

00:08:49.980 --> 00:08:53.490
And I think on a good day, that optimizes

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:55.353
for hyper-autonomy.

00:08:56.490 --> 00:08:59.280
There are other
considerations like the warmth

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:02.160
of camaraderie of the team being together,

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:05.400
sort of having an apprenticeship
for junior people.

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:07.890
Love to hear you talk a
little bit about the strengths

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:08.970
and weaknesses of that,

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:13.230
but really this idea of
conviction sort of governing all

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:16.410
and allowing people to
be hyper-autonomous.

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:20.700
- So I think in its lowest
order valence of conviction,

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:24.300
we're going after AWS or GCP or Microsoft

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:25.497
or anything else like that.

00:09:25.497 --> 00:09:27.000
And those are massive, right?

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:30.570
There's a standard set of trite
VC questions, which is like,

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:35.570
what if AWS and Google
and Microsoft do this?

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:38.430
And we're saying, "Yeah,
yeah, no, they already do this

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:40.830
and it's the most successful
business that there ever is,

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:43.047
and we're gonna go also do this."

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:49.220
And so I think you need to
play under very, very different

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:51.780
almost rules of engagement
with that because

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:53.910
they are the biggest
companies in the world.

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:55.920
The most important thing for them

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:58.290
is largely compute or data.

00:09:58.290 --> 00:10:01.827
We're trying to go not only go after them,

00:10:01.827 --> 00:10:04.230
but build what comes next essentially.

00:10:04.230 --> 00:10:06.600
And we're trying to do it
with less than 100 people.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:08.370
We're like 30 people right now.

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:11.460
And so for us to go
and be able to do that,

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:13.470
you have to take these higher kind

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:15.960
of conviction bets in these things

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:19.222
that maybe other people have
just completely written off.

00:10:19.222 --> 00:10:23.940
And so we take a deep amount
of care in customer support.

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:26.013
It's like a thing we care about a lot.

00:10:27.683 --> 00:10:29.940
We talk about it in terms of
working alongside the user.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:32.130
We talk about it in terms
of giving them the almost

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:33.270
like Railway experience.

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:34.590
If you've ever been to a nice restaurant

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:36.300
and they kind of cook in front of you?

00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:37.740
We want to give them that.

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:39.990
And it's like, okay,
but that has literally

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:43.020
nothing to do with selling software right?

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:45.030
But it does because it allows
you to actually move quicker

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:46.980
to close that loop in general.

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:49.350
We take a deep degree of design.

00:10:49.350 --> 00:10:53.700
Last generation of almost
like compute interaction was

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:57.390
mostly through Terraform, Helm,
Ansible, has nothing to do

00:10:57.390 --> 00:10:58.740
with the user interface.

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:01.230
And so you almost have
to stack these series

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:03.750
of deep conviction bets

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:05.070
and some of them are gonna be wrong,

00:11:05.070 --> 00:11:06.660
but you have to make
sure that you can kind

00:11:06.660 --> 00:11:08.610
of push on those outlier ranges

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:10.440
where almost nobody is investing.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:12.270
And you have to have
strong enough conviction

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:15.810
that you're right because
you're doing a bunch

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:17.430
of different things counterintuitively

00:11:17.430 --> 00:11:18.930
that other people are like, yeah, we just,

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:20.280
we don't put a lot of stock into that

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:21.810
because we don't think it matters.

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:25.170
- And one of the things I always sort

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:26.490
of marvel at in the way

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:30.210
that you've gone about
Railway is you decided to go

00:11:30.210 --> 00:11:34.200
after one of the biggest behemoths of all,

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:36.270
which is AWS in a sense.

00:11:36.270 --> 00:11:39.810
And sophisticated engineers
can still essentially use AWS

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:41.883
to do a lot of what you're doing.

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:44.310
And so in that sense,

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:47.700
it was fundamentally incredibly ambitious.

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:51.420
Some people call it the hardest
problem in DevOps overall.

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:54.180
How did you allocate your time between

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:57.150
building up sufficient
privacy and cybersecurity

00:11:57.150 --> 00:11:59.670
to make your customers comfortable

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:04.110
versus design and UX to make it fetching

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:08.040
and easy for customers
to use and way better,

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:10.980
10X better than alternatives out there,

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:14.490
but then at the same time,
actually build the product

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:17.580
to a level of integrity and competence

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:21.270
that you could credibly
have people trusting

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:24.367
to work with you versus more established

00:12:24.367 --> 00:12:26.160
$10 billion plus players?

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:28.740
- Yeah, I think it's a lot of trade-offs,

00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:30.750
but if you have a lot
of context on the space,

00:12:30.750 --> 00:12:32.910
you can make almost the right trade-offs.

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:34.560
You can almost think of
it more as like a graph

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:36.180
and you're trying to
decide what the right kind

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:37.860
of cut of the graph is.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:39.780
And so for us, we always want
to make sure that we do right

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:42.150
by the user from a perspective of

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:44.670
we take data very, very, very seriously.

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:46.950
We can never, ever, ever lose data.

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:48.333
That's a fundamental thing.

00:12:49.332 --> 00:12:50.730
It's like a fundamental
law of physics, basically.

00:12:50.730 --> 00:12:52.830
You can't undelete data.

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:56.520
So you need backups, you need
all of these other things,

00:12:56.520 --> 00:12:59.220
you need to make sure
that things are secure.

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:03.720
But at the same time, you
can't just completely deadhead

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:07.650
pure on security because
there are kind of benefits

00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:09.630
and trade-offs in terms of,
well, you could use that

00:13:09.630 --> 00:13:11.160
in terms of velocity

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:13.740
that we could build
differentiated experiences.

00:13:13.740 --> 00:13:16.090
And so we follow a loop in terms of almost

00:13:17.880 --> 00:13:19.920
What we call internally priority boarding.

00:13:19.920 --> 00:13:21.270
It's our beta program.

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:24.420
And so all of the kind of
hard and core that we have

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:26.250
of Railway is essentially,

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:30.960
it's not like a zero or
one shipping process.

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:32.550
It's kind of like a continuum, right?

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:33.990
So you start with something
that's in priority

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:36.180
boarding that's in beta.

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:37.860
It might have bugs,

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:39.570
it might have all these
other things, right?

00:13:39.570 --> 00:13:40.887
But as we work with those users

00:13:40.887 --> 00:13:43.770
and as we kind of build that
kind of like, hey, listen,

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:46.860
we'll work with alongside
you kind of experience,

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:48.390
we get to harden a lot of that, right?

00:13:48.390 --> 00:13:50.970
And we get to almost like
move up the value chain

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:52.410
with those users as we're going

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:54.388
and building those experiences, right?

00:13:54.388 --> 00:13:55.650
And so those new experiences,

00:13:55.650 --> 00:13:58.350
they'll start out on like
free users or like hobby users

00:13:58.350 --> 00:14:00.050
or anything else like that, right?

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:03.450
And we'll work with them, right?

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:05.490
And then over time as
those features get better

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:08.310
and better and better, we
move on to like the pro users

00:14:08.310 --> 00:14:09.750
or the enterprise users, right?

00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:11.970
And they have varying
different levels of constraints

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:15.109
that they will kind of like
roughly go towards, right?

00:14:15.109 --> 00:14:16.470
And so that allows us to
build those like features

00:14:16.470 --> 00:14:18.600
progressively, not just with the way

00:14:18.600 --> 00:14:20.100
that they're using the features,

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:22.830
but with the like constraints
sets that they need, right?

00:14:22.830 --> 00:14:24.960
Like maybe the users over
here on the enterprise

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:28.169
or Pro Plus side like need SOC2, right?

00:14:28.169 --> 00:14:30.630
And maybe Johnny Freeapp, Vibecoder

00:14:30.630 --> 00:14:32.040
doesn't even know what SOC2 is, right?

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:35.040
And so to apply this directly to this

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:36.780
is actually like you could use your energy

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:37.770
in various different ways.

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:40.410
- And speaking of, you know,
vibe coding, I look at,

00:14:40.410 --> 00:14:44.160
you know, Quadcode, for
example, which didn't exist

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:46.710
or have an equivalent
even a few years ago.

00:14:46.710 --> 00:14:47.543
- Yeah.
- And now it's

00:14:47.543 --> 00:14:49.440
improving a mile a minute.

00:14:49.440 --> 00:14:51.600
- Yep.
- Does that scare you

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:54.810
or is that just a massive accelerant

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:56.610
for what Railway's doing?

00:14:56.610 --> 00:15:00.513
- Yeah, I mean, lots of
thoughts there in general.

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:04.470
It's scary and it's anxiety inducing,

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:05.790
but it's also so exciting.

00:15:05.790 --> 00:15:07.470
There's just a massive, massive shift

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:10.140
in terms of what the models
are able to go in and do,

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:12.360
the rate at which, you
know, engineers are able

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:13.710
to go in and build.

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:14.940
Like you used to have this concept

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:17.040
of like a 10, 10X engineer, right?

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:19.140
And I think with these tools
now, you almost have this

00:15:19.140 --> 00:15:21.603
concept of like a 10,000
X engineer, right?

00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:26.130
And as a person building a
company, I think you have a kind

00:15:26.130 --> 00:15:29.850
of, you have a like duty
bound, honor bound thing

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:33.840
to make sure that if
your goal as a manager,

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:35.520
a leader or whatever is to attract

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:37.230
those like top talent people,

00:15:37.230 --> 00:15:38.910
your org needs to be
structured in such a way

00:15:38.910 --> 00:15:41.760
that those people like
actually can have the impact

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:43.740
and are rewarded for that impact

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:45.507
or they're gonna go somewhere else, right?

00:15:45.507 --> 00:15:47.640
And so it's just this crazy kind of thing

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:49.380
where it changes the entire continuum of

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:52.080
what people are able to go and build.

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:53.790
You have to like go out and like try

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:56.310
and use these tools really, really deeply.

00:15:56.310 --> 00:15:58.620
And my thesis is that, you know,
over the next little while,

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:02.040
like you're gonna get
to a point where you...

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:05.250
And I think arguably you're sort of there.

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:07.530
It's very difficult to find
the boundaries of these models

00:16:07.530 --> 00:16:10.320
and where they kind of
like break down now, right?

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:14.730
And I think that humans are
actually really, really bad

00:16:14.730 --> 00:16:17.070
at knowing like an exponential curve

00:16:17.070 --> 00:16:21.093
and comprehending what an
exponential does over time, right?

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:23.490
And like this is not an exponential,

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:25.050
this is like a super exponential, right?

00:16:25.050 --> 00:16:27.270
And so this rate is absolutely insane.

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:29.620
And so I think there's
just a massive, massive,

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:33.510
you call it an opportunity,
call it a shift,

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:34.860
call it like whatever it is,

00:16:34.860 --> 00:16:37.170
like something is happening there, right?

00:16:37.170 --> 00:16:39.180
And I think people should be
very, very aware that like,

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:41.250
you know in my mind, like,

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:43.710
and I've been an engineer
for the past like 10 years.

00:16:43.710 --> 00:16:46.530
I've probably been writing code for like,

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:47.820
like 15, almost like 20 now.

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:49.500
Like writing code by hand is dead.

00:16:49.500 --> 00:16:51.330
It's like, I'm happy to
like go ahead and say it.

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:53.460
Like I don't think you're
gonna write any code by hand

00:16:53.460 --> 00:16:56.310
like moving forward, like
providing these models stick.

00:16:56.310 --> 00:17:00.360
- I like to think that vibe coding

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:04.440
is almost tantamount with a
much higher rate of innovation

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:07.560
and accomplishment, which
should correlate with

00:17:07.560 --> 00:17:11.280
more instances of code
deployment, which should accrue

00:17:11.280 --> 00:17:12.900
to Railway's advantage.

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:15.390
Because essentially you're the receiving

00:17:15.390 --> 00:17:18.030
interface of all that activity.

00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:20.850
So what's fascinating when
you think about growth

00:17:20.850 --> 00:17:24.270
curves is, you know, you
always want to have a tailwind,

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:28.140
but if you have a tailwind
that's getting faster and faster

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:30.570
and faster, that's exciting

00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:31.800
as long as you're not gonna break.

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:33.600
- Yeah. Yeah, exactly right?

00:17:33.600 --> 00:17:35.970
And we've spent the last
like five years probably like

00:17:35.970 --> 00:17:37.620
building a bunch of these
foundational things,

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:40.350
like built an orchestration
engine from scratch.

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:42.480
We've built like a dashboard

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:45.000
for evolving changes over
time, like from scratch.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:46.653
So like kind of an equivalent of like Git.

00:17:46.653 --> 00:17:50.545
Like we've built a lot of these
things from scratch, right?

00:17:50.545 --> 00:17:51.570
And a lot of the next part of the journey

00:17:51.570 --> 00:17:53.700
is actually making sure
that those primitives

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:55.860
that we have for people
safely kind of like moving

00:17:55.860 --> 00:17:58.770
through time and iterating,
one, they only get better.

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:00.390
And two, they remain like safe, right?

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:02.640
Because provided you
can kind of be the kind

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:04.410
of like conduit for a
lot of this progress.

00:18:04.410 --> 00:18:06.300
Like I think there's this whole kind

00:18:06.300 --> 00:18:08.370
of like adage in the VC
community where they're like,

00:18:08.370 --> 00:18:11.040
oh, you know, value
crew's at the app layer,

00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:12.870
value crew's at the infrastructure layer.

00:18:12.870 --> 00:18:15.390
And like my thesis is value
crew's at the bottlenecks.

00:18:15.390 --> 00:18:16.710
Like you look at this

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:19.500
and you can kind of like navigate a lot of

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:21.060
where the markets are right now.

00:18:21.060 --> 00:18:24.060
Like Nvidia is a bottleneck,
Micron is a bottleneck.

00:18:24.060 --> 00:18:25.710
All of these things
are bottlenecks, right?

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:28.200
And when those, when this
kind of like force kind

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:30.450
of almost like pushes against
this manifold, you're gonna

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:32.460
see those things accumulate, right?

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:34.500
And so our thesis is internally,

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:36.150
we just don't want to be the bottleneck.

00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:37.380
We want to be this friction point

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:38.790
that like actually allows you to go in

00:18:38.790 --> 00:18:40.290
and push downstream, right?

00:18:40.290 --> 00:18:42.420
Because the more you can
kind of use this product

00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:45.210
and the more people
end up creating things,

00:18:45.210 --> 00:18:48.240
like we are purely incentivized
by our users winning,

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:49.560
which is what I love
about our pricing model.

00:18:49.560 --> 00:18:50.970
It's like purely usage based, right?

00:18:50.970 --> 00:18:53.520
So we're not gonna ding you on like seats

00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:54.660
or any of this other stuff.

00:18:54.660 --> 00:18:55.587
Like the more you use

00:18:55.587 --> 00:18:58.620
and the more you kind
of like create things,

00:18:58.620 --> 00:19:00.780
the more we're ultimately
successful as well, right?

00:19:00.780 --> 00:19:03.120
Which is super, super exciting.

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:05.610
But it's also like watching a, you know,

00:19:05.610 --> 00:19:06.960
like a freight train like come at you at

00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:08.130
like 500 miles an hour.

00:19:08.130 --> 00:19:09.990
You're like, oh my God,
like we have so much track

00:19:09.990 --> 00:19:11.670
to like lay, right, if this is the rate at

00:19:11.670 --> 00:19:13.292
which these things are moving, right?

00:19:13.292 --> 00:19:15.300
- I mean, the crazy
thing is for a while now

00:19:15.300 --> 00:19:16.650
it's almost become a cliche

00:19:16.650 --> 00:19:19.020
to talk about the 10X engineer.

00:19:19.020 --> 00:19:22.560
Which essentially means
that one engineer would be

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:25.470
as good or better than
10 regular engineers.

00:19:25.470 --> 00:19:30.470
But you are talking about a
10,000 X engineer these days.

00:19:30.720 --> 00:19:34.020
And in some ways that
makes a lot of sense.

00:19:34.020 --> 00:19:36.660
A really important question,
especially if you think

00:19:36.660 --> 00:19:39.540
that the best team wins,
which I generally do,

00:19:39.540 --> 00:19:41.640
is how do you recruit these people?

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:44.550
How do you find them?
How do you suss them out?

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:48.690
How do you cultivate them when they join

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:50.310
to achieve that potential?

00:19:50.310 --> 00:19:51.810
Because if there's actually such a thing

00:19:51.810 --> 00:19:56.810
as a 10,000 X engineer, if
you get them to only 20X,

00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:00.120
then you're missing out 500X.

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:01.320
- Yes, exactly. Right.

00:20:01.320 --> 00:20:04.770
- So tell me about your framework
on recruiting your tales

00:20:04.770 --> 00:20:08.430
from the trail of what's worked,
what hasn't, your go-tos.

00:20:08.430 --> 00:20:09.780
- We've always looked for
people who build something

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:11.013
of substance, right?

00:20:12.056 --> 00:20:13.830
And so like building marquee features

00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:15.750
or important parts of a product, right?

00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:18.367
Like one of our recruiters
internally says,

00:20:18.367 --> 00:20:19.980
"I don't care what company you worked at.

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:22.290
I care what you worked
on. Like was it important?

00:20:22.290 --> 00:20:24.360
Did people care? Was it interesting?

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:26.764
Like was there depth to
it in general?" Right?

00:20:26.764 --> 00:20:27.990
And I think that when you find people

00:20:27.990 --> 00:20:30.420
who care about these things

00:20:30.420 --> 00:20:32.970
and kind of like build these
like massive, you know,

00:20:32.970 --> 00:20:34.980
call them like cathedrals
or hyper structures

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:37.140
or whatever you want to call
them, they can talk about

00:20:37.140 --> 00:20:38.340
those forever, right?

00:20:38.340 --> 00:20:40.380
Like you can poke and you can push

00:20:40.380 --> 00:20:43.140
these people every layer down

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:44.190
and they're gonna have a really, really

00:20:44.190 --> 00:20:45.330
solid answer for that.

00:20:45.330 --> 00:20:46.830
And conversely, when you push people

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:49.260
and poke people that are masquerading

00:20:49.260 --> 00:20:50.520
or trying to like, you know, purport

00:20:50.520 --> 00:20:52.260
for somebody else's work, you
can actually watch it like

00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:53.820
fall apart in real time, right?

00:20:53.820 --> 00:20:55.620
They'll almost like backdate

00:20:55.620 --> 00:20:58.080
and be like, "Oh, well, that
was actually some other team

00:20:58.080 --> 00:20:59.367
in general, right?"

00:20:59.367 --> 00:21:00.960
And so I think sussing that out

00:21:00.960 --> 00:21:04.320
and looking for people who
have genuinely built things is

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:06.240
like really, really important, right?

00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:11.240
So like there's this conventional
advice in resumes where,

00:21:11.550 --> 00:21:14.580
you know, you'll say, oh,
you cut costs by like 75%

00:21:14.580 --> 00:21:17.580
or you like go and all of
these other things, right?

00:21:17.580 --> 00:21:20.190
And I call these zero calorie line items

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:22.243
because they don't actually
mean anything. It's like-

00:21:22.243 --> 00:21:23.580
- Well, you could have cut costs by 100%

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:25.050
by closing down the business altogether.

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:25.950
- Right, exactly right

00:21:25.950 --> 00:21:28.110
and so they don't actually mean anything

00:21:28.110 --> 00:21:30.060
and they surely don't tell me

00:21:30.060 --> 00:21:33.600
or like the observer anything
about what kind of work

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:37.110
that you care about and that
you need to be doing, right?

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:38.670
And I think another
thing that you can find

00:21:38.670 --> 00:21:40.020
with those people who actually really,

00:21:40.020 --> 00:21:41.970
really care about their craft

00:21:41.970 --> 00:21:43.680
or the things that they
want to go in and build,

00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:45.450
they have a really strong
through line for a lot

00:21:45.450 --> 00:21:47.070
of stuff they want to
go in and build, right?

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:49.200
Like the person who built
our data centers like

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:50.400
has been building up to this.

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:53.670
Like they built like bunch
of like physical hardware

00:21:53.670 --> 00:21:55.425
and all of these other things, right?

00:21:55.425 --> 00:21:57.025
And they're like, "I really
want to go in and do this."

00:21:57.025 --> 00:21:58.800
I was like, "Excellent,
you should do this here."

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:00.900
So I think sussing out those places

00:22:00.900 --> 00:22:03.630
where you have people who
are in it for, you know,

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:05.490
the love of the game or whatever, right?

00:22:05.490 --> 00:22:06.780
Like they're there, right?

00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:08.940
And those are the people that
we've really, really aimed

00:22:08.940 --> 00:22:11.100
to like go out and hire is like not people

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:12.510
that like want a job or anything.

00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:15.270
- Other than examining
marquee accomplishments,

00:22:15.270 --> 00:22:17.850
which makes a lot of sense,
do you have any other favorite

00:22:17.850 --> 00:22:20.670
interview questions or
ways of sort of examining

00:22:20.670 --> 00:22:22.890
what somebody's life has been about?

00:22:22.890 --> 00:22:27.420
- Yeah I like to ask
questions like, you know,

00:22:27.420 --> 00:22:29.940
around like what was the most
amount of risk you ever took?

00:22:29.940 --> 00:22:32.550
What was the hardest you ever worked?

00:22:32.550 --> 00:22:35.160
When is the biggest thing
something's ever backfired on you?

00:22:35.160 --> 00:22:36.750
Like what was your biggest like

00:22:36.750 --> 00:22:38.845
marquee success in your career?

00:22:38.845 --> 00:22:40.556
What do you want to go and do more of?

00:22:40.556 --> 00:22:42.037
All those other things.
- And so take something like

00:22:42.037 --> 00:22:43.350
what was the hardest
thing you ever worked on

00:22:43.350 --> 00:22:46.530
or what was the hardest
you ever worked rather?

00:22:46.530 --> 00:22:50.130
What's an example of how
you actually used that?

00:22:50.130 --> 00:22:52.350
- Yeah so I think what I like about

00:22:52.350 --> 00:22:54.460
that question is actually,

00:22:54.460 --> 00:22:56.520
I don't really care what the thing was.

00:22:56.520 --> 00:22:58.170
Like, I do actually, right?

00:22:58.170 --> 00:23:01.020
So, but it's like I 20% care
about what the thing was

00:23:01.020 --> 00:23:03.930
and I 80% care about like
why you did it, right?

00:23:03.930 --> 00:23:06.377
Like what was that thing
like worthwhile doing, right?

00:23:06.377 --> 00:23:09.690
And so I interviewed somebody
a little while ago, right?

00:23:09.690 --> 00:23:11.430
And I asked them about this stuff, right?

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:13.710
And they worked on some
pretty hard problems

00:23:13.710 --> 00:23:14.543
in general, right?

00:23:14.543 --> 00:23:17.520
But the one that they quoted
was pretty much right out of

00:23:17.520 --> 00:23:18.720
college, they worked with a bunch

00:23:18.720 --> 00:23:20.640
